<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#four-asian-tigers">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Four Asian Tigers</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Four Little Dragons</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Four Asian Tigers are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-korea">South Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>, economies that industrialised rapidly from the 1960s to the 1990s. Through export-led manufacturing, high savings and investment in education, they moved from poverty to high incomes within a generation. Their success became a model of development widely studied by other countries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/four-asian-tigers.jpg" alt="Four Asian Tigers" width="320" height="509" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Flag of Hong Kong.svg: Nightstallion and several others Flag of the Republic of China.svg: Nightstallion and several ... · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Four_Asian_Tigers_with_flags.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
